as monsoon season continues we're seeing  flood channels all across the valley  filled with raging water this can really  be dangerous and even deadly for the  homeless living there that's why a local  non-profit is working hard to get them  back on track jamie j ford has more on  their mission tonight  that's right there are more than 600  miles of underground tunnels like this  across clark county that are not safe  living conditions for the homeless just  last week at least two people died in a  flood channel after the heavy rains fell  across the city but shine a light a  local non-profit that goes into the  tunnels and rescues the homeless  population says their mission is to help  outreach director robert banhart tells  me he tries to help the displaced  because he was once living in the same  conditions i was attacked by three  people three people tried to kill me and  they did they left me for dead i woke up  three days later in the hospital bed and  when i was in that hospital china light  came into my life all these people had  been looking for me and all the people  i'd known in recovery my family had been  looking for me and that's he offered me  a way out china light was founded nine  years ago by author matt o'brien and  provides referrals to community partners  that help accommodate instant placement  with access to treatment and housing to  the people experiencing homelessness in  the greater las vegas area from las  vegas jeremy j ford live local now
 

